Z||000000||Dan010||10-28-2001||09:36 AM||||Did search, read haynes, still confussed!!<BR>EGR valve or EGR solenoid. Got code 32 after driving about 30 min. no other codes yet.<BR>I did clear codes and test drove, and again code 32<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||10-28-2001||10:20 AM||||More than likely the valve.  About $30 for a new ACDelco one (which is the kind you want to buy if you're going to replace it).||||reg||7
Z||000002||SLYGUY||10-28-2001||03:12 PM||||Make sure you replace it with GM. The aftermarket "universal" valve that I bought has the vacuum fitting on the opposite side. It was difficult to hook up, and the plastic hose heated up for some reason and cracked. I was able to mend it with some vacuum hose. Get the right one the first time. <BR>My universal one was the same price.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||10-28-2001||03:18 PM||||A code 32 is usually caused by a vacuum leak and is more of an annoyance than a serious problem.  <P>Check all of the vacuum connections associated with the EGR system.  The most common leak occurs at the hose that connects the EGR solenoid to the hard vacuum line attached to the firewall.  It is a regular rubber hose that deteriorates over time and either cracks or completely falls off.<P>I would check this out before spending any money on an EGR valve.  I'm not saying it doesn't need to be replaced, but a 50 cent piece of hose is much cheaper.<P>Also check the small hard plastic vacuum line attached to the EGR solenoid itself.....it is the one with the hard 90* bend in it and occasionally it will develop a crack right in the middle of the bend.<P>Good luck. ||||reg||1
